  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "Fall Storm Over Essex County",
    "overview": "Navy storm sky at dusk, an old mossy roof getting rained on, maple leaves blowing across it, a white aluminum eavestrough dripping at the edge. The visitor drags a finger across the roof and new shingles go down. The page is the flyer's palette (flag red, flyer gold, deep navy, flag-white paper) moved outdoors into the weather the business protects people from. It doesn't use stock house photos, blue quote banners or an icon-card services grid.\n\nType is heavy, uppercase and in the system stack, like a hand-set flyer: big, plain, confident. Away from the hero, the page settles into flat bands of colour (paper, navy, paper, red, deep navy). Rooflines and gutters are painted on canvas and join the bands, so the whole page reads as one house from ridge to eave. The density is low: one idea per band, large tap targets, and nothing a homeowner on a phone has to work for.\n\nMotion belongs to the world and never to the interface. Rain, leaves, drips and the re-shingling roof all run on canvas. They stay out of the words, and they come to rest as a finished still frame when the visitor asks for less motion.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Navy storm-sky hero with a live, painted shingle roof filling the bottom of the first screen",
      "Uppercase 900-weight system type for display and section heads",
      "Flat colour bands below the hero, joined by painted eavestroughs",
      "Gold is the one action colour; red is the flag, the leaf and the hover/focus signal",
